## Entry-point kinds

The input to **Rule 3' — a positive requires a reachability proof.** Before something is
documented as a capability, it must be traceable to one of these. Something reachable from
none of them is dead code, or a capability whose entry point you have not found — and which
of the two it is **is the finding**.

<!-- Replace with this project's real list. Examples:
     route · REST endpoint · scheduled job row · queue consumer · management command ·
     signal handler · webhook · public export · template tag · CLI subcommand -->

- {{ENTRY_POINT_KIND}}

**String-named references live here too.** Anything named by a string rather than a symbol
(a scheduler row naming a dotted path, a provider registry key) is invisible to grep and to
the type-checker, so it looks dead and is not. List those mechanisms explicitly.

## Observation instrument

How a claim is promoted to `seen`.

| Need | Instrument here | Constraint |
|---|---|---|
| Observe a surface state | <!-- dev browser, read-only --> | <!-- e.g. auth throttle ~20 navigations/hour --> |
| Exercise a write path | <!-- isolated test DB / E2E runner --> | **Never dev, never prod** |
| Run a code path | <!-- probe against the isolated test database --> | Counts as `seen` |

**Read-only against dev; anything that writes goes to the isolated environment.** A
write-path claim verified against dev has contaminated the fixture the next reader will
check it against.

